                            {
                                "target_word": "grove",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "bdb61397-26e9-382e-98f1-827b13b21cab",
                                        "entry_id": "c176d55d-0d64-3f44-941e-81b2a0531280",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To cultivate in groves; to grow naturally so as to form groves."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "456ce295-36f2-4b65-8289-95ae133773f7",
                                                "sense_id": "bdb61397-26e9-382e-98f1-827b13b21cab",
                                                "text": "It is called \"Orchard Lake,\" from the fact, that near the centre is an island embracing an area of about fifty acres of land, well groved with different kinds of shrubbery; and near the centre of this island stand a number of aged apple-trees, planted, perhaps, a century since by the hand of some Indian."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4d6a4413-a4e2-4971-9c12-7f461c002512",
                                                "sense_id": "bdb61397-26e9-382e-98f1-827b13b21cab",
                                                "text": "The trees and shrubs are not arranged after any particular system, but are scattered or groved together in various parts of the garden."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a1003de4-dac3-4669-9f5c-10852fcc9d5c",
                                                "sense_id": "bdb61397-26e9-382e-98f1-827b13b21cab",
                                                "text": "1984, Queensland Botany Bulletin, Issue 3, Department of Primary Industries, page 82,\nVirtually recognizable groving occurs in some A. aneura associations in the west. Further east some diffuse groving may occur, but is difficult to recognize without the benefit of aerial photographs."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9d3a31e8-aa35-39ed-a527-fff9d00a7a9f",
                                        "entry_id": "c176d55d-0d64-3f44-941e-81b2a0531280",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To cultivate with periodic harvesting that also serves to create order (gaps and lines of trees) to facilitate further harvesting."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "of trees"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5bafc9af-1bf8-4ba0-bc70-fca65090ede1",
                                                "sense_id": "9d3a31e8-aa35-39ed-a527-fff9d00a7a9f",
                                                "text": "In Herefordshire, especially on the northern and eastern sides, Oak timber abounds; and in many of the woods it is usual to have felling at periods varying from sixteen to twenty years; the straightest and handsomest are left for timber, or, as it is called, groved; and they are from time to time thinned, and a regular distance kept between them. The effect produced on these groved trees is, that from being exposed to air and sun, the rapidity of their growth is increased in bulk, height, and quality; and in sixty or eighty years they become valuable timber."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "05f0d97f-137b-35bd-865b-586047ac8182",
                                        "entry_id": "c176d55d-0d64-3f44-941e-81b2a0531280",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To plough or gouge with lines."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e2c345bc-180d-4933-a92f-100e1037554a",
                                                "sense_id": "05f0d97f-137b-35bd-865b-586047ac8182",
                                                "text": "1823, Instinct, in \"Sholto and Reuben Percy\" (Thomas Byerley), The Percy Anecdotes: Original and Select, Volume 9: Instinct—Ingenuity, page 138,\nVery frequently, however, to shorten the distance to the upper nurseries, where they have to take the eggs, they project an arch of about ten inches in length, and half an inch in breadth, groved or worked into steps, on its upper surface, to allow of a more easy passage."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "481a8514-7647-40b6-a2a7-9e7d5853b834",
                                                "sense_id": "05f0d97f-137b-35bd-865b-586047ac8182",
                                                "text": "The floor of first story and piazza to be laid with Georgia pine, in narrow courses planed, groved and tongued, and laid in the best manner."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ac90687d-e974-4ea9-a8b2-d9d2870c2b44",
                        "source_entry_id": "5d7048c9-e0d8-3a9b-a4cd-71d74c709756",
                        "source_sense_description": "petit bois",
                        "target_lang_word": "shaw",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "shaw",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b1a1da14-50eb-3db6-b329-2eb13bc9fb14",
                                        "entry_id": "d484530b-18b4-3ba0-bccd-09bdecd8f09b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A thicket; a small wood or grove."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "dated",
                                            "dialectal"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b1f1684b-f666-4b95-81ea-9e9186b4d1e7",
                                                "sense_id": "b1a1da14-50eb-3db6-b329-2eb13bc9fb14",
                                                "text": "All this herd sire Lamorak / and on the morne sir lamorak took his hors and rode vnto the forest / and there he mette with two knyghtes houynge vnder the wood shawe",
                                                "translation": "All this heard Sir Lamorak, and on the morn Sir Lamorak took his horse and rode unto the forest, and there he met with two knights hoving under the wood-shaw",
                                                "english": "All this heard Sir Lamorak, and on the morn Sir Lamorak took his horse and rode unto the forest, and there he met with two knights hoving under the wood-shaw"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f708536b-ee46-45ee-8e5b-33d5af0076c8",
                                                "sense_id": "b1a1da14-50eb-3db6-b329-2eb13bc9fb14",
                                                "text": "Thenne said sire kay I requyre you lete vs preue this aduenture / I shal not fayle you said sir Gaherys / and soo they rode that tyme tyl a lake / that was that tyme called the peryllous lake / And there they abode vnder the shawe of the wood",
                                                "translation": "Then said Sir Kay: I require you let us prove this adventure. I shall not fail you, said Sir Gaheris. And so they rode that time to a lake that was that time called the Perilous Lake, and there they abode under the shaw of the wood",
                                                "english": "Then said Sir Kay: I require you let us prove this adventure. I shall not fail you, said Sir Gaheris. And so they rode that time to a lake that was that time called the Perilous Lake, and there they abode under the shaw of the wood"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "712a1dfe-a6ea-4ce0-8f9d-7e33c9979e2f",
                                                "sense_id": "b1a1da14-50eb-3db6-b329-2eb13bc9fb14",
                                                "text": "The snows are fled away, leaves on the shaws, / And grasses in the mead renew their birth,"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b9c76a03-5dfd-314b-8548-b131a10ef42f",
                                        "entry_id": "d484530b-18b4-3ba0-bccd-09bdecd8f09b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The leaves and tops of vegetables, especially potatoes and turnips."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Scotland"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "21dfa465-e83d-4f4e-a856-89330741317c",
                                                "sense_id": "b9c76a03-5dfd-314b-8548-b131a10ef42f",
                                                "text": "Up here the hills were brave with the beauty and the heat of it, but the hayfield was still all a crackling dryness and in the potato park beyond the biggings the shaws drooped red and rusty already."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
